What is Inheritance?
•	Inheritance allows a class (child/derived class) to reuse properties and methods of another class (parent/base class).
•	It helps in code "reusability" and "method extension".

Types of Inheritance in Python:
1.	Single Inheritance -> One child class inherits from one parent class.
2.	Multilevel Inheritance -> A class inherits from another derived class.
3.	Hierarchical Inheritance -> Multiple child classes inherit from the same parent.
4.	Multiple Inheritance -> A child class inherits from more than one parent class.


Multilevel Inheritance:-
        💡 Key Rule (Very Important)
        ⭐ Create object of LAST child to access ALL features
        👉 Parent object → only parent features
        👉 Child object → parent + child
        👉 GrandChild object → parent + child + grandchild

Multiple Inheritance:-
        🏠 Real-Life Analogy
        👨 Father + 👩 Mother → 👶 Child
        Child inherits traits from BOTH parents ❤️

    Method search order important
        Python uses:
        ⭐ MRO — Method Resolution Order
        👉 Left → Right order
        If same method exists in both parents:
        👉 ParentA first (because written first)

    ⚠️ LIMITATIONS / CORE ISSUES
    🔴 1) Method Name Conflict
            If both parents have same method:
                class ParentA:
                    def show(self): ...
                class ParentB:
                    def show(self): ...

                class Child(ParentA, ParentB):
    🤔 Which show() will run?
        👉 Python will call:
        ⭐ ParentA.show()
        Because Python follows left → right order (MRO — Method Resolution Order)

    🏆 When to Use Multiple Inheritance?
        ✔️ Combine features from different sources
        ✔️ Mix behaviors
        ✔️ Framework-level design
        ✔️ Advanced OOP systems

Hierarchical Inheritance:-
    📘 Hierarchical Inheritance — Quick Interview Revision

    Hierarchical Inheritance is an OOP concept where multiple child classes inherit from a single parent class.

    👉 Structure:
        One Parent → Many Children

        👉 Syntax Example:
        class Parent:
        ...

        class Child1(Parent):
        ...

        class Child2(Parent):
        ...

    Key Points:
        • One base class is shared by multiple derived classes
        • All child classes inherit common features from the parent
        • Each child can also have its own unique features
        • Child classes are independent of each other
        • Parent cannot access child features

    Method Access:
        Each child object can access:
        ✔️ Parent methods and variables
        ✔️ Its own methods and variables
        ❌ Other child class features

    Advantages:
        ✔️ Eliminates duplicate code across similar classes
        ✔️ Ensures consistency via a common parent
        ✔️ Easy to extend new child classes
        ✔️ Promotes modular design

    Limitations:
        ⚠️ Changes in parent affect all child classes
        ⚠️ Tight coupling between parent and children
        ⚠️ Cannot share child-specific behavior between siblings
        ⚠️ Poor design may lead to an oversized parent class

    Solutions / Best Practices:
        • Keep parent class generic and stable
        • Avoid putting child-specific logic in parent
        • Use composition when sharing behavior between children
        • Apply Single Responsibility Principle

    Real-life analogy:
    A single parent having multiple children — all inherit common traits but each child is unique.

    In short:
    Hierarchical Inheritance enables multiple classes to reuse common functionality from a single base class while remaining independent.

